"""Render the iterative extension from the public tables, without separate hand-entered numbers."""
import csv
from pathlib import Path
from html import escape
OUT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]/'output'
def lead():
    with (OUT/'iterative-wsp-endpoints.csv').open() as f:
        rows={(r['task'],r['size'],r['seed'],r['method']):r for r in csv.DictReader(f) if r['split']=='outer'}
    cases={(t,w,s) for t,w,s,m in rows}; assert len(cases)==87
    def val(k,m):return float(rows[k+(m,)]['raw_rmse'])
    delta=[val(k,'iter_examples')-val(k,'examples') for k in cases]
    return (f"With examples only, allowing up to three revisions lowered outer-test error in {sum(x<0 for x in delta)} "
            f"of 87 settings, left it unchanged in {sum(x==0 for x in delta)}, and raised it in {sum(x>0 for x in delta)}. "
            "The comparisons below distinguish exact 64-bit answers from small numerical errors.")
def section():
    with (OUT/'iterative-wsp-by-size.csv').open() as f:rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))
    names={'square':'Square','sin':'Sine','selfpower':'Positive self-power','tanh':'Hyperbolic tangent','add':'Addition','subtract':'Subtraction','sum':'Running sum','fib':'Fibonacci','tri':'Triangular numbers','sort':'Sort four'}
    order={'examples':0,'iter_examples':1,'formula':2,'iter_formula':3,'gd':4,'gd_lbfgs':5,'adam_bfgs':6,'ls_xavier':7,'pool_gd':8,'pool_gd_lbfgs':9}
    def h(t):return escape(str(t),quote=True)
    chunks=[]
    for t,title in names.items():
        data=[r for r in rows if r['task']==t and r['split']=='outer' and r['method'] in order]
        tr=''.join('<tr>'+''.join('<td>'+h(x)+'</td>' for x in [r['size'],r['params'],r['method_name'],str(int(float(r['median_exact_count'])))+' / '+r['n_examples_per_start'],format(float(r['median_raw_rmse']),'.4g')])+'</tr>' for r in sorted(data,key=lambda r:(int(r['size']),order[r['method']])))
        chunks.append('<details><summary>'+h(title)+'</summary><table><thead><tr><th>Size</th><th>Parameters</th><th>Final weights</th><th>Exact examples</th><th>Raw RMSE</th></tr></thead><tbody>'+tr+'</tbody></table></details>')
    return '''<h2>Summed extrapolation error</h2><p>For each task we average raw root mean squared error over its tested network sizes and three random starts, then sum the ten task averages. The chart ranks all 14 complete optimizer pipelines. The values depend on target magnitude, with Fibonacci dominating most totals.</p><img src="figure-1-all-optimizers.png" alt="All fourteen optimizer pipelines ranked by summed extrapolation error" style="width:100%;height:auto"><p><a href="all-optimizer-raw-rmse-ranking.csv">Full totals and ranks</a> · <a href="optimizer-rmse-task-contributions.csv">Contributions by task</a> · <a href="architecture-choice-models.csv">New architecture choices</a> · <a href="architecture-choice-costs.csv">Measured inference costs</a></p><h2>Recorded training progress</h2><p>Round zero is the original one-shot weight-space programming (WSP) reply. Development data select revisions; their curve is not an untouched test. Stopped runs retain their last selected weights.</p><img src="iterative-wsp-training-curves.png" alt="Observed training, development and untouched extrapolation error across WSP revision opportunities" style="width:100%;height:auto"><p><a href="iterative-wsp-learning-curves.csv">All recorded selected states</a> · <a href="iterative-wsp-proposal-curves.png">Accepted and rejected proposals</a> · <a href="iterative-wsp-task-training-curves.png">Training curves for every task</a></p><h2>The functions computed by the weights</h2><p>These fixed examples use the first random start and sixteen hidden units. Shading identifies the central training and development interval; curves show frozen predictions throughout the deployment domain.</p><img src="function-prediction-curves.png" alt="Actual square sine and hyperbolic tangent predictions inside and outside the training interval" style="width:100%;height:auto"><h2 id="iteration">Does a second look at the weights help?</h2><p>Each iterative weight-space programming (WSP) run begins with its original one-shot answer. Astra can revise the weights at most three times using training and development diagnostics. Each size stops on exact training and development predictions, after two unaccepted revisions, or at the third attempt. Every reported outer input was hidden from the feedback loop. Full-batch gradient descent using backpropagation and the same endpoint refined with limited-memory Broyden–Fletcher–Goldfarb–Shanno (L-BFGS) are scored separately. The last two rows at each size also give these optimizers the full development pool as extra labeled training data.</p><p>Open a task to see its median across three starts. Exact counts require all coordinates of a prediction to match their 64-bit references. Root mean squared error (RMSE) is in target units. <a href="iterative-wsp-by-size.csv">All sizes and three splits</a> · <a href="iterative-wsp-endpoints.csv">Individual starts, feedback and independent confirmation</a> · <a href="pdf/weight-space-programming-public.pdf">Revised paper</a> · <a href="table-4-charts.png">Table 4</a> · <a href="table-3-task-charts.html">Explore the charts for each task</a>.</p>'''+''.join(chunks)
